f any immediate meld. So maybe discarding T4 is the best option. That would free up space and allow me to draw a T3 or T5 if possible. Alternatively, discarding J1 or J3 to get a pair.\n\nBut considering that I need a pair for the winning hand, maybe discarding J1 or J3 is better. Let's say I discard J1. Then, if I draw another J1, I can form a pair. But I need a pair in the winning hand. So maybe that's a better move.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T4, I can still form the T7-T8-T9 Chow, and maybe find another meld with the remaining tiles.\n\nHmm. It's a tough call. Let's think about the points. If I can form the T7-T8-T9 Chow, that's one meld. Then, maybe another meld. For example, if I have W2 and W7, but not helpful. Alternatively, maybe the J1 and J3 can form a pair if I get another one. But that's not a meld.\n\nAnother angle: What's the most probable way to win? If I discard T4, maybe I can get a T3 to form a T2-T3-T4 Chow. If I get that, then I have two Chows (B1-B2-B3 and T2-T3-T4, T7-T8-T9). Then, remaining tiles would be W2, W7, B6, J1, J3. Need another meld and a pair. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard J1, then have J3. If I get another J1, that's a pair. Then, with the two Chows, maybe I can win. But I need four melds and a pair. So two Chows plus two more melds and a pair.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe the safest move is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. T4 is a candidate. So I'll choose to discard T4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has a meld of [B1, B2, B3] and tiles T7, T8, T9 which can form another Chow. Discarding T4, which is not part of any immediate meld, frees up space to potentially form additional Chows or pairs.  \nReason: T4 is a single tile with no clear path to forming a meld, while retaining T7, T8, T9 increases chances of completing a Chow. Discarding T4 allows flexibility to draw T3 or T5 for future Chows.  \nAnswer: Play T4"}
